// @test: `our<T>?()` constructs an absent optional owned handle (#427 defect 3, #471)
// @description: an absent optional could not be constructed for an owned handle: `our<Int>?()`
//   and `our<A>?()` were rejected in expression position ("Expected primary expression.
//   (found '?')"), although `Int?()`, a struct (`A?()`) and `mild<Int>?()` all parsed. The
//   expression parser skips its type-prefixed construction path for the ownership keywords
//   (`my`/`their`/`our`/`borrow`/`view`) whenever the identifier is followed by a call, but the
//   skip was keyed on the *name* alone -- so `our<Int>?(` never reached the type parser, whose
//   `?` postfix is what builds the OptionalType. The skip now requires the call form, so the
//   keyword reaches the same path as every other type name. Both the absent and the present arm
//   are asserted: an `our<A>?()` matches `?`, and a handle returned from a call matches the value.
//   The grouped spellings are asserted too (#471): a parenthesized type parses as a single-member
//   tuple, so `(T)?` used to build OptionalType(TupleType(T)) -- a type not equal to `T?`, refused
//   with "Expected T? but got (T)?". The one-element group is now unwrapped before the optional
//   wrapper, so `(Int)?()` / `(our<A>)?()` construct the same absent optionals as `Int?()` /
//   `our<A>?()`. A multi-member tuple (`(Int, Int)?`) is left as the tuple.
// @category: ownership, parser
// @expect-output: empty=2 present=4 grouped=0 tuple=1
// @expect-return: 0

share(all)
struct A { v<Int> }

maybe(b<Bool>)<our<A>?> -> {
    if (b) {
        return our(A { v: 4 })
    }
    return our<A>?()
}

main()<Int> -> {
    e<our<A>?> = our<A>?()
    absent<Int> = 0
    match (e) { v -> { absent = 1 } ? -> { absent = 2 } }

    f<our<A>?> = maybe(true)
    present<Int> = 0
    match (f) { v -> { present = v.v } ? -> { present = -1 } }

    # grouped spellings (#471): `(T)?()` must be the same type as `T?`
    gg<Int?> = (Int)?()
    gh<our<A>?> = (our<A>)?()
    gp<mild<A>?> = (mild<A>)?()
    grouped<Int> = 0
    match (gg) { v -> { grouped = grouped + 100 } ? -> { } }
    match (gh) { v -> { grouped = grouped + 10 } ? -> { } }
    match (gp) { v -> { grouped = grouped + 1 } ? -> { } }

    tt<(Int, Int)?> = (Int, Int)?()
    tuple<Int> = 0
    match (tt) { v -> { tuple = -1 } ? -> { tuple = 1 } }

    println("empty=" + absent.to_string() + " present=" + present.to_string()
        + " grouped=" + grouped.to_string() + " tuple=" + tuple.to_string())
    return 0
}
